            <ArticleTitle>Neurocysticercosis presenting as a vertical one-and-a-half syndrome with associated contralesional horizontal gaze paresis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>250-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jns.2012.08.022</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-510X(12)00471-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe a patient presenting with vertical one-and-a-half syndrome and concomitant contralesional horizontal gaze paresis as the result of a solitary neurocysticercosis (NCC) lesion in the right midbrain extending into the thalamomesencephalic junction. The patient received an albendazole-dexamethasone course which resulted in resolution of his symptoms. The neuro-ophthalmological complications of NCC are reviewed and the clinical topography of the neuro-ophthalmological findings of this unusual observation are discussed.</AbstractText>
